for months during the rainy season large  parts of Bangladesh are regularly under  water the large rivers overflow and  flood huge areas often ferocious  cyclones destroyed people's homes and  farmland their livelihoods and their  lives at times like this the longed-for  rain becomes a curse a trip along the  Brahmaputra River shows the extent of  the erosion the floodwaters eat into the  land parts of the bank break off  disappearing into the river but the  country's population of 160 million need  every bit of arable land ko Kamiya who  is over 70 years old has seen many  floods in his lifetime once he was a  wealthy farmer with his own rice fields  he has 6 sons and several grandchildren  but repeated flooding made it harder and  harder to feed his family 11 times the  floods have taken everything away my  fields my house 11 times we had to move  and start over again then came the next  flood and destroyed everything over and  over again 11 times two years ago we  ended up here the floods have turned us  into refugees the landless farmers have  found ways of cultivating these sandy  sediments along the river they dig pits  fertilize them with cow manure and plant  pumpkins  since the thick shelled squash keep for  a long time they can be sold at a profit  during the barren winter months simple  but clever strategies like these are  needed if Bangladesh is to meet the  challenges of climate change near mile  Chandra BAP re is agriculture manager  for the Bangladesh branch of the charity  practical action he's convinced that  planting suitable crops in the sandbars  will help the most impoverished  Bangladeshis cope with climate change  then I think it will be Menace then when  the first storms announced the start of  the monsoon period people will be able  to look to the future with more optimism
 

